Device batch command outcomes
=============================

Batch reboot and firmware requests dispatch each unique device ID at most once
within that request. Organization, site permission, role/API-key ceilings and
confirmation checks retain their existing behavior. An inaccessible device or
one without a usable controller session is not attempted.

Every eligible device receives one result, even when controller cleanup fails.
The existing device_id/success/message keys remain, with success always boolean.
The added outcome distinguishes accepted, rejected, unknown and not_attempted.
Accepted requires an AdapterResult with success exactly True: it means the
adapter acknowledged the command, not that hardware execution was verified.
Rejected requires a recognized policy/capability refusal. Malformed responses,
unclassified failures and exceptions after invocation are unknown; check device
state before retrying. Arbitrary provider messages and exception text are not
returned or included in this handler's warnings.

Cleanup errors cannot turn an accepted result into a second failure result or
cause an automatic retry. A fixed warning accompanies retained outcomes. Failed
context entry attempts disconnect. Cancellation propagates even if context exit
raises another error or suppresses it; interrupted batches do not report normal
completion. There is no automatic command replay.

Qualification includes actual PostgreSQL rows, HTTP-ASGI routes and separate
processes exercising TCP loopback HTTP. Cancellation is tested by direct handler
invocation, explicitly not as client-disconnect behavior. Authenticated principals
and controller adapters are synthetic; no vendor hardware is changed. Checks
cover duplicate IDs, mixed access, missing controllers, success/refusal, malformed
results, sensitive diagnostics, setup/entry/exit failures and cancellation.

This is request-local duplicate prevention, not cross-request idempotency or
exactly-once hardware execution. It does not verify hardware completion, actual
login, concurrent authority revocation, controller ownership/lifecycle consistency,
or third-party adapter logs. No frontend batch caller was identified; the endpoint
behavior is qualified independently. Full shipping/runtime checks are separately
gated and recorded; these checks alone do not establish enterprise readiness.
